> On 30 November 2012 12:24, Dennis Murphy <djm...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> HI Ross:
>>
>> On Thu, Nov 29, 2012 at 4:55 PM, Ross Gayler <r.ga...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> > Hi Dennis,
>> >
>> > Thanks for that (I'm at the bottom of the plyr learning curve). In terms
>> > of
>> > your example code, I'm trying to do something slightly different - I'm
>> > trying to include the fit object (your m) in the data frame so that I
>> > can do
>> > things with it later.  I had assumed that one of the columns of the data
>> > frame could be a vector of objects of class "lm". I suspect this is a
>> > basic
>> > misunderstanding of what is possible with a data frame.
>>
>> The problem is that model objects are generally lists, and method
>> functions (e.g., summary(), predict() and residuals()) exist for
>> specific types of model objects. For example, summary.glm() is not
>> exactly the same as summary.lm(). Moreover, for a given model object
>> m, summary(m) is a different object than m itself.
>>
>> The advantage of list objects is that each component can be a
>> different type of object with different lengths/dimensions. You can
>> see this by fitting a model object, let's call it m, and then calling
>> str(m). Data frames are more restrictive: each column has the same
>> number of rows, and certain types of objects create problems in data
>> frames (e.g., lists and language objects). The bottom line is that
>> lists are more efficient in terms of storage, but at the cost of
>> increased complexity. The game is learning how to efficiently extract
>> information from lists, and that's where knowing how to write
>> extraction functions becomes useful.
>>
>> >
>> > My current solution is to accumulate all the descriptive attributes of
>> > the
>> > models in a data frame and have a separate list of the models.
>>
>> That's fine; my main point was that you should first create a list of
>> models and then write one or more functions to extract 'similar' types
>> of information from each model object in the list. My example showed
>> exactly that for estimated coefficients and R^2 for a sequence of
>> linear models whose model objects were organized into a list of lists,
>> the output from each model call comprising a list. Notice that in my
>> example, the coefficients came from the model object per se but the
>> R^2 value came from the object returned by summary(model_object). What
>> would be more wasteful of space would be to write a function that, for
>> a given model object, returns a data frame of, for example, model
>> coefficients, R^2, residuals, fitted values and responses; the latter
>> three variables would be of length n, which would require that the
>> model coefficients and R^2 be replicated n times to conform to the
>> requirements of a data frame. This is why I mentioned that you might
>> want to write multiple extraction functions for different types of
>> (related) output.
>>
>> The row index
>> > into the data frame and the list is the same so I can retrieve the model
>> > and
>> > the associated attributes by looking up the data frame and list.
>>
>> Do you mean attributes of the model or attributes of the model object?
>> One can assign attributes to R objects, so you have to be a little
>> careful with the terminology. Look at the bottom of str(model_object)
>> to see what I mean.
